AUX-CONTEXT|<work>/refute-out/run/aux_src_single_sided_lp_AbstractSingleSidedLP_sol_tokenizeWithdrawRequest_255.txt
Tracing the actual flow: tokenizeWithdrawRequest is reached only from __postLiquidation, i.e. strictly after the
  liquidation's health/valuation step, so it cannot change what the health check saw in that same call. Its body
  iterates TOKENS(), skips any token with a zero-address manager, and forwards to
  manager.tokenizeWithdrawRequest(liquidateAccount, liquidator, sharesToLiquidator); the in-file comment and the ||
  didTokenize accumulation make explicit that this is a noop when there is no existing withdraw request — it
  splits/transfers a pre-existing request to the liquidator, it does not mint one. The only code path in either file
  that creates a request is __initiateWithdraw → BaseLPLib.initiateWithdraw, which is driven by the position holder's
  own action and which first calls _unstakeAndExitPool on the corresponding yieldTokenAmount before calling
  manager.initiateWithdraw ("Will revert if there is already a pending withdraw"). So the LP is unstaked and exited
  before a request can exist — the premise that the escrow branch "omits still-staked Curve/Convex LP" has no
  corresponding state: a pending request implies those yield tokens already left the pool.

  The valuation branch itself is also not the loose read the claim needs. convertToAssets only diverts when
  t_CurrentAccount != address(0) && _isWithdrawRequestPending(t_CurrentAccount), and hasPendingWithdrawals returns true
  only if some manager reports w.requestId != 0 for that account under this vault. If it does divert,
  getWithdrawRequestValue loops every token in TOKENS() and executes require(hasRequest) for each manager — explicitly
  commented "Ensure that this is true so that we do not lose any value" — so a partial/asymmetric request state reverts
  rather than under-reporting.

  For the claimed exploit to hold, an unprivileged liquidator would have to make hasPendingWithdrawals(victim) flip from
  false to true within the liquidation transaction and before valuation. Neither shown file offers that: the only
  request-creating call is gated behind the account's own withdraw initiation, and the liquidator-reachable entry point
  runs post-liquidation and is a documented noop absent a request. The derived CurveConvex2Token overrides only
  NUM_TOKENS, PRIMARY_INDEX, TOKENS, _totalPoolSupply, and _checkReentrancyContext — none of which touch request
  creation or the valuation branch condition. The manager implementation is not shown, and the tie-break rule sends
  unshown-code dependence to REFUTED regardless.

  CONSTRAINT|C21|A claim that an attacker can force an alternate valuation branch must identify the concrete state
  transition that flips the branch predicate and show it is reachable by the attacker before the valuation is read,
  rather than pointing at a post-check hook whose own code documents it as a no-op when that state is absent.
  VERDICT|REFUTED|src/single-sided-lp/AbstractSingleSidedLP.sol:tokenizeWithdrawRequest:255|C21|The tokenize path runs
  only in __postLiquidation (after the health check) and is a documented no-op unless the account already has a request,
  which only the account's own __initiateWithdraw can create — and that path unstakes/exits the LP first, so the
  liquidator can neither flip hasPendingWithdrawals mid-check nor leave staked LP unvalued.
